A||||12||2birds||Slipping clutch! , any new ideas for parts wanted.||||9||
Z||000000||2birds||07-19-2000||10:40 PM||||Today, my clutch (RAM, only around 45,000 miles on it) started slipping in 5th gear, when I mashed the gas around 45 mph.  I had just got off the interstate after a little 30 mile blast.  After the car sat for a few hours, it wouldn't slip anymore.  Anybody else ever see this before?  The last two times I replaced it, once it started to slip it would do it all the time. They both lasted around 60,000 miles. <P>My flywheel has already been turned twice.  Anybody know what the minimum thickness is, or what a new flywheel costs if I need it?<P>I changed it myself last time, but due to time constraints, I'm not looking forward to it again (Sea duty sucks).  This time I think I'm going to drop the whole cradle and driverain as an assembly, and pull the motor off of the transaxle.  I'd much rather do the lineup work on the floor instead of overhead.  Might even port my manifolds while I'm at it.||||reg||9
Z||000001||SeanyD||07-20-2000||01:23 AM||||I'm about to go through the whole clutch replacement on my car...just a quick question...the flywheel being turned...did you do that optionally, or does it need to be done and why||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroshop||07-20-2000||01:34 AM||||Check out my site and scroll down to see the clutch. This is a kevlar clutch specially made for the shop and we carry a double your previously miles warranty, meaning that if you got 45000 miles on your last clutch then the warranty is 90000 miles. One item of importance, if you do not resurface the flywheel then your warranty is null and void. A flywheel can be turned 3 to 4 times before needing replacement. Also a word to the wise. I installed a brand new flywheel and did not resurfaced the unit and ended up pulling the clutch back out to resurface the brand new flywheel. Oh well! live and learn. If you need more info call me. My tel: # is on my web site. <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.net" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroshop.net</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||PJ||07-20-2000||07:53 AM||||Cutting the flywheel too thin doesn't cause the clutch to slip. It causes it not to release. The only way to be sure is to measure it. Or shim it the same amount that the machine shop takes off. <P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||07-20-2000||08:02 AM||||Hey 2Birds-<P>I was a bubblehead myself many years ago-<BR>Missile Tech (yes, a steely-eyed-baby-killer)<BR>USS Andrew Jackson, SSBN 619- Gold crew<BR>We were tendered next to the Hunley in Scotland. (that should tell you almost HOW long ago it was!)<BR>Anchors away!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Taiji<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||07-21-2000||12:42 AM||||Thanks for the replies.  I'll probably stick with the Ram setup, since it only cost me about $140 from Summitt 4 years ago.  I only got around 45,000 miles, but I thrash it pretty hard most of the time, and I've let some friends drive it a few times.<P>Has anybody else ever changed their clutch by dropping the whole cradle and drivetrain as an assembly?  I made a brace to hold the engine up last time, and it was still a real PITA.<P>I'm still thinking of other things to do while the drivetrain is out.  I did cradle bushings and oil pan gasket/rear main seal last time.  <P>Hey Taijiguy, I'm content to push, make water for showers, and make electricity for movies with my hot rock.  15 down and five 'til retirement!  1 attack boat (Hyman G. Rickover, SSN-709, Norfolk) and one boomer (Louisiana, SSBN-743 Blue, Kings Bay) in twenty years.  <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 07-21-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000006||Ed||07-21-2000||01:20 AM||||Regarding the clutch slipping:<P>When you take it apart make sure there is no oil getting on the clutch.  My rear cam seal failed (V-6) and oiled the clutch at about 110,000 miles.  Otherwise, the stock clutch still had an adequate amount of facing.  <P>For the experts: Can the rear main fail and cause the same failure?  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||07-21-2000||01:31 AM||||I would think it would be hard for the oil to get past the flywheel with the wheel spinning, but I've heard people say it happens.  My last disk looked good, but it was real glazed, and the flywheel was highly polished.  I think the pressure plate springs wear out more than the disk.<BR>I'm afraid to get any higher clamping force because I already have nough axial crankshaft movement when I push in the clutch pedal.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||01-31-2001||03:10 PM||||I know this is an old topic, but I wanted to start by searching the old posts.  I need a clutch for my 88 Formula, but from what I've found, no one has really recommended anything that wasn't a race only clutch.  I checked out clutchnet.com, but the only heavy duty clutch I saw wasn't for Fieros.  Lots of race-only, but not HD street.  I was thinking Centerforce, but from what some people of said, now I'm not so sure.  Also, the RAM clutch from the Fiero Store...I've never heard of them, and the references I've seen online don't seem that great.  I'd like to get more then 50K mi. out of my clutch.  The car has 108K on it now, but I don't know if it's the original clutch or not.  What is the average lifespan of a good clutch in a Fiero, anyway?  I know it varies with driving conditions, but on average...<BR>Sorry for the long post, but I need some help soon. I just bought my baby on Monday, and I can't really drive it till I get the clutch replaced.  Talk about driving me crazy! (she is driveable, but the clutch chatters too much - throwout bearing?)  Also, are the cross-drilled rotors at the Fiero Store worth over 2x the price of the OEM style rotors?  I need to do brakes, but don't want to waste money.  Suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000009||2birds||01-31-2001||05:36 PM||||I just went with an OEM replacement from a local parts vendor (Dealer's Equipment in Jacksonville, FL).  Pretty much identical to the GM and RAM parts I've pulled out. $110 for parts plus $20 to resurface the flywheel.  No complaints in about 2000 miles.  <BR>I don't race it, but I do drive it pretty hard, so I don't mind only 50-60,000 miles, for the money.  If I was nice to it, I'd probably get beter mileage.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||02-03-2001||01:47 PM||||When I did my clutch recently,I left the motor in place,and dropped the cradle and tranny;took a day.Jackstands were placed in front of the engine compartment,and a support was used to hold the engine in place.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||02-03-2001||07:28 PM||||I never even considered changing the clutch on my Formula without dropping the cradle. First time I ran into lots of rusted bolts.<P>The second time I replaced mine(the ATF fiasco) it only took me about 5 hours. I think it was 2 hours to drop the cradle assembly, 1 hour of clutch swapping and re-aligning, and another 2 hours to put the cradle assembly back in.||||reg||1
Z||000012||PJ||02-03-2001||11:48 PM||||Ram sells a  kevlar disk with a stock pressure plate. I went to this set-up after wiping clean the organic disks a couple of times. Hooks up great once it is warm and it won't self-destruct when you beat on it. Down sides are a little clutch shudder and it will wear down the flywheel. I think the distance crank end to flywheel surface is about .8 inch. I can check this if you need to know it. Cross drilled rotors look cool but are a waste of money. Especially on the vented '88 set-up.||||reg||1
